# Job runner with leases
intent: Workers claim queued jobs under a 30 second lease and renew it while they work. A job whose lease lapses goes back to the queue, and fails for good after three attempts.

+ type Job
    id: string
    state: queued | running | done | failed
    leaseOwner?: string
    leaseUntil?: timestamp
    attempts: int

+ machine Job
    states: queued, running, done, failed
    initial: queued
    final: done, failed
    queued -> running  on a worker claims it
    running -> done  on the worker finishes
    running -> queued  on the lease lapses  after 30s
    running -> failed  on the lease lapses  if attempts reached 3
    * -> failed  on an operator cancels it  [external]

service worker
+ file worker/run.ts
  + fn claimNext(workerId) -> Job | null  [entry]
      job = oldest queued job, locked for update  [db:jobs]
      if no job: return null
      transition Job to running
      write leaseOwner, leaseUntil = now + 30s, attempts + 1  [db:jobs] [ttl:30s]
      return job
  + fn runJob(job, workerId)  [entry]
      parallel:
          -> heartbeat(job, workerId)
          result = do the work against the target  [network:target-api] [timeout:120s]
      -> finish(job, result)
  + fn heartbeat(job, workerId)
      every 10s:
          extend leaseUntil to now + 30s where leaseOwner is this worker  [db:jobs] [ttl:30s]
  + fn finish(job, result)
      ?? WHY decides whether a worker that was frozen for a while can overwrite a job it no longer owns | ASSUMED finish trusts its caller | ALT finish writes only where leaseOwner is still this worker, and drops the result otherwise
      transition Job to done
      store the result and clear the lease  [db:jobs]

service reaper
+ file reaper/sweep.ts
  + fn sweep()  [entry]
      every 5s:
          for each running job whose leaseUntil has passed:  [db:jobs]
              if attempts reached 3:
                  transition Job to failed
              else:
                  transition Job to queued
              clear the lease  [db:jobs]

trace Happy path
  claimNext > oldest queued | job j_1
  claimNext > transition Job to running |
  claimNext > write leaseOwner | lease: w_A until 0:30
  runJob > heartbeat |
  heartbeat > extend leaseUntil | +10s: lease: w_A until 0:40
  heartbeat > extend leaseUntil | +10s: lease: w_A until 0:50
  runJob > finish | +3s: work returned
  finish > transition Job to done |
  finish > store the result | j_1 done, lease cleared

trace A worker freezes for 45 seconds
  claimNext > transition Job to running | w_A claims j_1
  claimNext > write leaseOwner | lease: w_A until 0:30
  runJob > do the work | +45s: w_A sat in a long pause, so its heartbeat never ran
  sweep > for each running job | the lease lapsed 15 seconds ago
  sweep > transition Job to queued | j_1 is back in the queue, attempts = 1
  claimNext > transition Job to running | +1s: w_B claims j_1
  claimNext > write leaseOwner | lease: w_B until 1:16
  finish > transition Job to done | ! +4s: w_A wakes up and finishes. Nothing checks that w_A still holds the lease
  finish > store the result | ! w_A's result is stored and w_B's lease is cleared while w_B is still working

not doing:
  - no priority queues
  - no per-job lease length

decided:
  - 2026-09-12 | run.claimNext | how long a job can sit with a dead worker before someone else may take it | chose: a 30s lease renewed every 10s | over: a 5 minute lease with no heartbeat: simpler, but a crashed worker blocks its job for 5 minutes
